# Accessibility (WCAG 2.2 AA)

## Foundations

- Semantic HTML first. ARIA only when native semantics are insufficient.
- First rule of ARIA: do not use ARIA to fix bad markup if a native element exists.
- All functionality must be keyboard operable with logical tab order matching reading order.
- Visible focus indicators are mandatory. Never remove outlines without an equal or better `:focus-visible` replacement.
- Buttons perform actions; links navigate. Do not fake either with non-accessible clickable divs.
- Icon-only controls need accessible names (`aria-label` or visually hidden text).

## Focus and Keyboard

- Avoid keyboard traps. Dialogs may trap focus, but must restore focus on close.
- Focused elements must not be fully obscured by sticky headers, toasts, cookie bars, or overlays.
- Prefer `tabindex="0"` or `-1` only. Do not use positive tabindex values.
- Custom widgets (tabs, menus, comboboxes, disclosure) must follow WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices or use battle-tested primitives (Radix, React Aria, Base UI).

## Forms and Feedback

- Inputs need labels, descriptions, error association, and correct types/autocomplete.
- Status messages/toasts should be announced accessibly without unnecessary noise.
- Visible label text should be included in the accessible name.
- Authentication and forms must not rely on cognitive puzzles when alternatives exist.

## Contrast and Perception

- Text contrast >= 4.5:1 (large text >= 3:1).
- UI components, icons, and focus indicators >= 3:1 against adjacent colors.
- Do not convey meaning by color alone.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for non-essential animation.
- Images need meaningful alt text, empty alt for decorative images, and captions/transcripts for media when needed.

## Targets and Robustness

- Interactive targets should be at least ~24x24 CSS px; prefer larger for primary touch controls.
- Keep name, role, and value synchronized with visual state (`aria-expanded`, `aria-selected`, `aria-pressed`, etc.).
- Do not hide focusable elements with `aria-hidden="true"`.
- Prefer accessible component primitives over hand-rolled widgets for complex interactions.
